= Context

The Academy of Integrated Science (AIS) has shifted from a program-focused model to a student-centric one in which collegiate faculty teach across multiple programs. Because they are housed within the Academy rather than in a single department, they can move between program needs and support cross-disciplinary courses without the boundary constraints that tenure-line faculty face.

Enrollment growth has widened the capacity gap. We need additional collegiate faculty to maintain course coverage and support new offerings.

In Nanomedicine, Dr. Chixia Tian's departure has left a significant gap. Dr. Penghui Zhao covers some courses as a post-doctoral researcher, but this is temporary. Although we are searching for one replacement, a single hire will leave the program with limited flexibility. A second line would expand upper-division course offerings in clinical medicine, biotechnology, and pharmaceuticals, and provide biology and chemistry coverage for the Integrated Science Curriculum.

The Science, Technology, and Law (STL) minor is also growing rapidly, yet only one full-time faculty member, James Steiner, J.D., is devoted to it. An additional line would be essential to sustain that growth.

This plan requests one additional collegiate faculty line in Nanomedicine, beyond the search already underway, and one collegiate line in STL.

= Hiring Plan Overview
This hiring plan is in two parts:
+ *Immediate hires* (highest priority):
  - _Nanomedicine Major_: One additional collegiate faculty line.
+ *Potential near-future hires*:
  - _Science, Technology, and Law Minor (STL)_: One collegiate faculty line (Assistant Collegiate Professor or Assistant Professor of Practice).

= Instructional Needs
*Nanomedicine:*

Upper-division Nanomedicine coursework has been covered primarily by Dr. Carla Finkielstein (Department of Biological Sciences) and Dr. Chixia Tian (AIS). Since Dr. Tian left, the program has faced a coverage gap in lab-based and medically oriented topics, which Dr. Zhao has covered temporarily as a post-doc. The search currently underway will replace Dr. Tian, but even with that hire, the program still needs greater diversity in course offerings, particularly in clinical medicine, biotechnology, and pharmaceuticals, and additional capacity for the Integrated Science Curriculum in biology and chemistry. Both the new hire from the current search and the holder of the additional requested position would be expected to teach in the ISC program.

Dr. Finkielstein remains the only other faculty member regularly covering upper-division Nanomedicine. Because her appointment is in the Department of Biological Sciences and no formal MOU guarantees her continued teaching in AIS, the program is vulnerable if her departmental obligations shift. A permanent, AIS-based collegiate faculty lines would provide the stability that a temporary post-doc cannot.

*Science, Technology, and Law (STL):*

The STL minor continues to grow. James Steiner, J.D., the sole full-time faculty member, is supported by adjunct David Cockrum. With only one full-time line, the program depends on a single person and cannot absorb further growth. An additional faculty line would allow the program to expand beyond its current core offerings and provide law and policy courses for students across other AIS programs. Possible new courses include Trademark Law, Entertainment Law, and Name-Image-Likeness (NIL) law, which would support students heading to law school or IP-related careers.

= Specific Requests
== 1. Nanomedicine (Highest Priority)
We request *one additional Assistant/Associate Collegiate Professor* beyond the search already underway. The position would cover upper-division Nanomedicine courses, including lab-based and clinical topics, and teach in the ISC program in chemistry and/or biology. Ideally, the new hire would also maintain links with the Fralin Biomedical Research Institute to enhance research and internship opportunities for students.

== 2. Science, Technology, and Law (Highest Priority)
We request *one new Assistant Collegiate Professor or Assistant Professor of Practice* to expand STL's offerings and protect continuity of instruction. The position would teach core STL courses and develop new offerings such as Trademark Law, Entertainment Law, and NIL topics. The additional line would also support the extension of the STL minor and the 3+3 initiative with George Mason University's law school.

= Conclusion
These requests address immediate staffing needs in Nanomedicine and STL. Additional collegiate faculty lines will allow AIS to maintain course coverage, protect against single-person contingencies, and support continued enrollment growth.
